{"name":"Permafrost","hex":"#98eff9","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"permafrost","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Permanently frozen ground, or a specific layer thereof."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/permafrost"}],"entries":[{"title":"Permafrost","blocks":[{"text":"Permafrost is soil or underwater sediment which continuously remains below 0 °C (32 °F) for two years or more; the oldest permafrost has been continuously frozen for around 700,000 years. Whilst the shallowest permafrost has a vertical extent of below a meter, the deepest is greater than 1,500 m (4,900 ft). Similarly, the area of individual permafrost zones may be limited to narrow mountain summits or extend across vast Arctic regions. The ground beneath glaciers and ice sheets is not usually defined as permafrost, so on land, permafrost is generally located beneath a so-called active layer of soil which freezes and thaws depending on the season."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Circum-Arctic_Map_of_Permafrost_and_Ground_Ice_Conditions.png/330px-Circum-Arctic_Map_of_Permafrost_and_Ground_Ice_Conditions.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permafrost","wikidata":"Q179918"},{"title":"Permafrost (story)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Permafrost\" is a science fiction novelette by American writer Roger Zelazny, published in 1986."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permafrost_(story)","wikidata":"Q7169194"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Vertical_Temperature_Profile_in_Permafrost_%28English_Text%29.jpg/500px-Vertical_Temperature_Profile_in_Permafrost_%28English_Text%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Vertical_Temperature_Profile_in_Permafrost_%28English_Text%29.jpg/500px-Vertical_Temperature_Profile_in_Permafrost_%28English_Text%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Permafrost temperature profile. Permafrost occupies the middle zone, with the active layer above it, while geothermal activity keeps the lowest layer above freezing. The vertical 0 °C or 32 °F line denotes the average annual temperature that is crucial for the upper and lower limit of the permafrost zone, while the red lines represent seasonal temperature changes and seasonal temperature extremes. Solid curved lines at the top show seasonal maximum and minimum temperatures in the active layer, while the red dotted-to-solid line depicts the average temperature profile with depth of soil in a permafrost region.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVertical_Temperature_Profile_in_Permafrost_(English_Text).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Digging_in_permafrost.jpg/500px-Digging_in_permafrost.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Digging_in_permafrost.jpg/1280px-Digging_in_permafrost.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Digging_in_permafrost.jpg/500px-Digging_in_permafrost.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Excavating ice-rich permafrost with a jackhammer in Alaska.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADigging_in_permafrost.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Sayedi_2020_post_LGM_permafrost.jpg/500px-Sayedi_2020_post_LGM_permafrost.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Sayedi_2020_post_LGM_permafrost.jpg/500px-Sayedi_2020_post_LGM_permafrost.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn